# --- system administration ---
 sudo, openssh-server, openssh-client, curl, wget, git, vim, nano, tmux,
# libpam-pwquality enforces the password-quality floor (policy shipped in
# /etc/security/pwquality.conf.d) — a hard dep so it can't be silently dropped.
 libpam-pwquality,
 screen, tree, less, bash-completion, rsync, lsof, strace, procps, psmisc,
# --- networking ---
 net-tools, iproute2, iputils-ping, dnsutils, mtr-tiny, traceroute, tcpdump,
# nftables: both product pages advertise it and sysible-cli's own firewall check
# looks for it by name, but nothing installed it -- so `sysible verify` reported
# "no firewall configured" on a fresh install of a product sold as having one.
 nftables,
 nmap, netcat-openbsd, iperf3, ethtool,
# --- archives ---
 unzip, zip, bzip2, gzip, xz-utils, p7zip-full,
# --- troubleshooting ---
 htop, iotop, sysstat, ncdu, smartmontools, lm-sensors,
# --- modern CLI ---
 jq, ripgrep, fd-find, bat, fzf,
# --- languages / dev base ---
 python3, python3-pip, python3-venv, pipx, build-essential,
# --- automation & IaC (from Debian where present; the rest via sysible-release) ---
 ansible, ansible-lint,
# sshpass is what lets Ansible use a PASSWORD over SSH at all. Without it
# `--ask-pass` / ansible_password does not degrade — it refuses outright with
# "to use the 'ssh' connection type with passwords, you must install the sshpass
# program", which is exactly the first run against a fresh host that has no key
# on it yet. A hard dep: the bootstrap run is the one that cannot go and fetch a
# missing package first.
 sshpass,
# --- containers ---
# Docker Engine + Compose are HARD dependencies, not Recommends. Every Sysible
# product runs as a container and is brought up with compose, so an install
# without them cannot run the software it was installed to run — which is exactly
# what happened: a machine reached an operator who was then told by the installer
# to go and fetch Docker themselves.
#
# They sat in Recommends because they come from download.docker.com rather than
# Debian, and a Recommends meant "a missing upstream repo never blocks the whole
# install". The cost of that safety was that the one thing the products cannot do
# without was the one thing nothing guaranteed: docker-ce is excluded from the
# generated ISO toolkit list by the VEND filter in live-build/build.sh, the vendor
# hook was the only thing installing it, and no verify step checked the result.
#
# Depends is only safe because the ISO build now configures Docker's apt repo
# through live-build's config/archives, which is set up BEFORE packages are
# installed. This .deb is installed from config/packages.chroot during that same
# phase, so without the archive the dependency would be unresolvable and the ISO
# build would fail outright. build.sh writes both the list and the (fingerprint-
# checked) key; tests/test_docker_is_part_of_the_default_install.py pins all of it
# together so the three cannot drift apart again.
 docker-ce, docker-compose-plugin, containerd.io,
# podman stays alongside, and is not redundant: it is in Debian proper, so it is
# the runtime that is present even when the Docker repo is unreachable, and
# install-sysible falls back to it (with podman-docker) on a machine where
# docker-ce could not be installed.
 podman, buildah, skopeo,
# --- Sysible platform prerequisites ---
# openssl: SLOP's installer mints five 64-hex secrets with `openssl rand -hex 32`
# (the SSO shared secret, the updater secret, the Baseline gateway secret and
# agent token, and the Flashback token). It has a /dev/urandom fallback, but the
# platform the image exists to run must not depend on a fallback firing, and
# `install-sysible slop` is one of the two questions the installer asks. It was a
# Recommends, which apt installs by default and --no-install-recommends silently
# drops -- and dropping it leaves the gateway unable to mint its own secrets.
 openssl,
# --- data & config wrangling --------------------------------------------
# yq is the YAML half of jq, and it was simply absent. Everything an operator
# edits on this image is YAML -- compose files, Kubernetes manifests, Ansible
# playbooks, CI workflows -- so the image shipped the JSON tool and not the
# YAML one. yamllint is what rejects a broken manifest before it is applied;
# jo builds JSON from the shell the way jq reads it.
 yq, yamllint, jo,
# --- shell engineering ---------------------------------------------------
# shellcheck is already part of this project's OWN workflow: scripts in this
# repository carry `# shellcheck disable=` directives, so the tool that reads
# them belongs on the image that ships them. shfmt formats what it checks.
 shellcheck, shfmt, pre-commit,
# moreutils (sponge/ts/chronic/ifne) and GNU parallel are what server-side
# pipelines reach for; entr re-runs on change, hyperfine times properly,
# expect drives a prompt that has no batch mode, and at is cron's one-shot
# counterpart. pv and dos2unix are the everyday two.
 moreutils, parallel, entr, hyperfine, expect, at, pv, dos2unix,
# --- git -----------------------------------------------------------------
# git is a hard dep above; git-lfs is what makes a repository with binary
# assets clone at all, and its absence looks like a corrupt checkout.
 git-lfs, tig,
# --- network debugging ---------------------------------------------------
# socat is what port-forwarding, socket relaying and "is that unix socket
# answering" actually use. netcat-openbsd above does not cover any of it.
 socat, whois, httpie,
# tshark carries the dissectors tcpdump's output does not, and conntrack is
# how Docker's and nftables' NAT is inspected -- the first thing to look at
# when a published container port does not answer.
 tshark, conntrack, fping, ipcalc, nethogs, ngrep, bmon,
# --- VPN / reaching a management network ---------------------------------
# An image sold for managing fleets shipped no VPN tooling whatsoever.
 wireguard-tools, openvpn,
# --- hardware inventory --------------------------------------------------
# The Sysible Controller reports on host hardware, and smartmontools plus
# lm-sensors were the only things on the image that could see any of it.
# dmidecode/lshw/lspci/lsusb are how a host answers "what am I", nvme-cli and
# hdparm how its disks do, and ipmitool how a server is reached out-of-band
# when it is not booted.
 dmidecode, lshw, pciutils, usbutils, nvme-cli, hdparm, ipmitool,
# --- performance & burn-in -----------------------------------------------
 fio, stress-ng, sysbench, numactl, bpftrace, linux-perf, gdb,
# --- backup & object storage ---------------------------------------------
# rclone talks to every object store an engineer meets; restic is a real
# deduplicating backup tool, which sysiblectl's own tarballs are not.
 rclone, restic, s3cmd,
# --- database clients ----------------------------------------------------
 pgcli, mycli,
# --- the Python libraries Ansible's cloud modules import -----------------
# ansible is a hard dep above, but its AWS and Kubernetes modules are not
# self-contained: they import boto3 and the kubernetes client AT RUNTIME and
# fail with "Failed to import the required Python library" without them.
# Shipping ansible without these ships the half of it that cannot reach a
# cloud -- and the failure surfaces on the first play, not at install time.
 python3-boto3, python3-kubernetes,
# --- shell & everyday quality of life ------------------------------------
 zsh, direnv, zoxide, tealdeer,
Recommends:
# Heavier pieces — installed by default, removable with --no-install-recommends.
# This is the Sysible Server toolset MINUS the GUI: no GNOME/Calamares, no
# desktop apps (VSCodium, Wireshark, virt-manager, LibreOffice, CherryTree,
# SysTerm), no open-vm-tools-desktop — only the headless server pieces remain.
# VM guest integration (folder sharing etc. sans the desktop bits).
 open-vm-tools,
# languages (bigger toolchains)
 golang, rustc, cargo, nodejs, npm, default-jdk, maven, gradle,
# databases (clients)
 postgresql-client, default-mysql-client, sqlite3, redis-tools,
# security
 gnupg, fail2ban, auditd, apparmor, lynis,
# monitoring / CLI. All three are in bookworm MAIN on amd64 and arm64
# (verified against the package indices), so the old note about
# contrib/backports did not apply to the suite this image is built from --
# and 'Recommends keeps them soft' would not have saved the build either,
# for the reason spelled out at eza below.
 btop, atop, iftop,
# eza is not in bookworm at all (verified against the bookworm package
# indices for amd64 and arm64). The field it sits in is NOT what keeps the
# build alive: build.sh expands Depends, Recommends AND Suggests into one
# live-build package list, which apt installs as a hard set. Being in
# build.sh's VEND list is the only reason eza never reaches that list, and
# the vendor hook installs its binary instead. Treat 'Recommends' here as
# documentation of intent for an apt-installed machine, not as build safety.
 eza,
# headless virtualization host (the GUI virt-manager/virt-viewer are dropped)
 libvirt-daemon-system, qemu-system, qemu-utils,
# libvirt-clients carries virsh. It used to arrive only as a Recommends of
# libvirt-daemon-system (or, on the Workstation, of virt-manager), so the one
# command that drives a hypervisor was two levels of "probably" deep while
# SLEP's hypervisor feature assumes it. Named explicitly instead.
 libvirt-clients,
# These come from third-party repos wired up by sysible-release; Recommends so a
# missing upstream repo never blocks the whole install:
 kubectl, helm, k9s,
 opentofu,
 awscli, sops, age,
 gh,
# azure-cli and google-cloud-cli were in Suggests, which apt does NOT install.
# That made the metadata disagree with the build in two directions: the vendor
# hook installs both unconditionally, so every ISO HAS them, while a machine
# that installed sysible-server from repo.sysible.com got neither -- two
# different toolsets from one product name. Recommends is the field that
# matches what actually happens, and it is safe here for the same reason the
# rest of this list is: both are in build.sh's VEND set, so neither reaches
# the generated toolkit list and the ISO build never has to resolve them.
 azure-cli, google-cloud-cli,
Suggests:
 sysible-controller, sysible-agent,
Description: Sysible Server — headless engineering platform (all-in-one)
 Turns a Debian base into the Sysible Server: the tools a Linux/DevOps/SRE/platform
 engineer reaches for, installed and ready on first boot, so there is no half-hour
 of apt after every fresh install — the same toolset as Sysible Server but
 headless (no GNOME desktop, no Calamares, no GUI applications).
 .
 Depends carries the must-haves (shell, networking, the container runtime and
 compose that every Sysible product runs on, the modern CLI set, Python, Ansible,
 the Sysible CLI). Recommends carries the heavier pieces (Kubernetes, OpenTofu,
 cloud CLIs, headless libvirt/QEMU, the language toolchains) so a lean build can
 drop them with --no-install-recommends.
 .
 Tools that are not in Debian (Docker CE, kubectl/helm, OpenTofu, cloud CLIs) are
 pulled from their upstream apt repositories, which sysible-release configures — so
 `apt install` Just Works without vendoring their binaries.
